1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
America's first superhighway.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
A ribbon of water carved through sheer rock and untamed wilderness.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Featuring an ingenious network of stone locks and aqueducts.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Skeptics called it Clinton's Ditch,

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
but visionaries saw it as the gateway to a gleaming future.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, the Erie Canal on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
In the woods off Interstate 90 in eastern New York, there's a

8
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:40,000
narrow ditch surrounded by a tangle of weeds.

9
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:43,000
It's a rare remnant of one of America's greatest engineering

10
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:47,000
achievements. I got a mule and her name is Sal

11
00:00:47,001 --> 00:00:52,000
15 miles down the Erie Canal She's a good old worker and a good

12
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:55,000
old man. She's a good old worker and a

13
00:00:55,001 --> 00:01:00,000
She's a good old worker and a good old pal.

14
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:03,000
Celebrated in story and song, the Erie Canal

15
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:05,940
first opened for business in 1825.

16
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:08,340
It was the first commercial link between New York

17
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:09,840
and the American West.

18
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:15,860
Well, the Erie Canal was a breakthrough public works

19
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:16,960
project.

20
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:19,400
It was the most mammoth project ever attempted by this country

21
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:20,460
at the time.

22
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:23,000
It was sweeping in its grandeur.

23
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:26,540
It was a canal for our nation, not just a canal for New York

24
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:27,740
State.

25
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:31,840
And in a very real sense, it united the country in ways

26
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:35,020
that no other event of its time could do.

27
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:39,480
It was indeed a world famous engineering marvel.

28
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:41,480
One analogy I like to make is that it's very much

29
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:44,580
like the internet today, that people really viewed it

30
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:47,020
as something that was amazing, that was unprecedented, that

31
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:50,500
was very much changing their lives.

32
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:52,960
From Albany, New York City,

33
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,740
on the Hudson River, the Erie Canal stretched

34
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:57,800
through a gap between the Appalachian Mountains

35
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:00,940
on the south and the Adirondacks on the north.

36
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:08,540
363 miles later, it terminated in Buffalo, linking the Great

37
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:11,260
Lakes with the eastern seaboard.

38
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:13,960
It opened up the entire heartland of the United States

39
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:16,860
to commerce not only with the eastern seaboard,

40
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:17,860
but with the world.

41
00:02:17,861 --> 00:02:23,260
Since its completion in the early 19th century,

42
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:26,600
the Erie Canal has undergone three separate reconstructions

43
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:28,600
and upgrades.

44
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:32,700
The original waterway was just 40 feet wide and 4 feet deep,

45
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,240
but it was big enough to consolidate the strength

46
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:37,780
of a young nation.

47
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:42,680
Its parade of traffic traveled literally at a mule's pace.

48
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:45,480
Led by a young boy called a hoagie,

49
00:02:45,481 --> 00:02:48,180
The mules walked a path along the canal

50
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:52,120
and towed boats crowded with cargo and passengers.

51
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:55,220
There would have been a tow path on this side of the canal.

52
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:57,220
It would have been perfectly quiet.

53
00:02:57,320 --> 00:03:01,220
All that one would be hearing, perhaps, here,

54
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:06,840
would be the hoof sounds of the towing animals,

55
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:09,340
and the boat would silently glide along.

56
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:14,540
Boats traveling west on the Erie Canal

57
00:03:14,541 --> 00:03:19,540
carried manufactured goods from the industrialized east.

58
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:23,540
Supplies ranging from axes and shovels to dishware and clothing

59
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:25,880
helped settlers develop the new frontier.

60
00:03:29,380 --> 00:03:32,200
At the same time, the canal made the rich land and resources

61
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:37,000
of the west accessible to cities east of the Appalachians.

62
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:39,540
Goods such as flour, wood, and pelts

63
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:43,340
filled the canal boats bound for Albany.

64
00:03:43,341 --> 00:03:46,640
Once at the capital, the boats were rafted together and towed

65
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:51,380
by steamboats down the Hudson River to New York City.

66
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:53,720
There, supplies were transferred onto larger,

67
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:58,160
ocean-going vessels bound for ports around the globe.

68
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:01,720
But the canal boats carried more than just provisions.

69
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:04,860
They carried the dreams of countless immigrants who

70
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:08,600
hoped to find their fortunes in the bountiful West.

71
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:10,460
There was a river of humanity that

72
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:12,100
came from Europe to the East Coast

73
00:04:12,101 --> 00:04:16,100
and then flowed along the Erie Canal to this port.

74
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:19,140
And then from here, some people stayed.

75
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:21,640
Others went farther west on schooners

76
00:04:21,740 --> 00:04:23,540
and settled the heartlands of the country.

77
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:25,920
A lot of Americans had ancestors who

78
00:04:26,020 --> 00:04:30,080
stood in this piece of land.

79
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:32,480
Before the canal was built, the only way west

80
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:35,560
for pioneers and merchants was an arduous and expensive trek

81
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:37,460
by wagon.

82
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:41,360
For them, the Erie Canal was a dream come true.

83
00:04:41,361 --> 00:04:44,260
The canal basically took a six-week trip by wagon

84
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:47,360
down to about six to 10 days once it was complete

85
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:48,360
across the state.

86
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:50,640
And then from that point in Buffalo,

87
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:53,140
you could go all the way across the Great Lakes as far

88
00:04:53,240 --> 00:04:54,340
as Chicago by water.

89
00:04:57,200 --> 00:05:00,040
Today, the canal is still busy with traffic.

90
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:02,080
But the boats are usually carrying tourists

91
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:03,680
instead of freight.

92
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:06,580
It really gives them a sense of history.

93
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:08,880
I mean, there's new locks and there's other canals.

94
00:05:08,881 --> 00:05:13,020
canals, but if you figure how far this dates back, it gives

95
00:05:13,021 --> 00:05:16,420
them a very interesting perspective on travel, you know,

96
00:05:16,421 --> 00:05:19,920
from our 500-mile-an-hour jet planes that everybody's used to

97
00:05:19,921 --> 00:05:22,720
to on the Erie Canal and the locks is quite a different

98
00:05:22,721 --> 00:05:23,421
perspective.

99
00:05:27,140 --> 00:05:30,540
Back in the 1780s, almost as soon as the ink was dry on

100
00:05:30,541 --> 00:05:34,640
the US Constitution, the new nation began to look for an

101
00:05:34,641 --> 00:05:38,540
economical way to push west, to tap the rich resources

102
00:05:38,541 --> 00:05:40,940
available in the Western territories.

103
00:05:40,941 --> 00:05:43,820
But access to resources wasn't the only reason that the US

104
00:05:43,821 --> 00:05:46,480
wanted to link the regions.

105
00:05:46,481 --> 00:05:50,380
It just wasn't economics, because there was a genuine fear

106
00:05:50,381 --> 00:05:53,460
by George Washington that if we don't unite these people that

107
00:05:53,461 --> 00:05:56,220
are moving into the interior with the Eastern Seaboard,

108
00:05:56,221 --> 00:05:57,760
they might trade with Canada.

109
00:05:57,761 --> 00:05:59,720
And they didn't want that economic tie,

110
00:05:59,721 --> 00:06:02,940
because maybe there would be a political tie.

111
00:06:02,941 --> 00:06:04,800
But America's dreams of expansion

112
00:06:04,801 --> 00:06:10,300
formidable obstacle, the Appalachian Mountains.

113
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:12,600
The mountain range that stretches from the southern tip

114
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:17,040
of the Smokies to as far north as Maine was a daunting natural

115
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:22,860
barrier that had slowed Western migration for decades.

116
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:25,960
The American West was simply cut off from the Atlantic Coast

117
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:30,260
cities, except at one location.

118
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:33,760
Fortunately for New York State, as the Appalachian Mountains

119
00:06:33,761 --> 00:06:37,400
move northward from Alabama, they veer in Pennsylvania east

120
00:06:37,401 --> 00:06:40,440
and then continue north again in what is now primarily

121
00:06:40,441 --> 00:06:42,900
New England.

122
00:06:42,901 --> 00:06:45,600
This twist in the mountain chain creates a gorge,

123
00:06:45,601 --> 00:06:48,000
which allows the Hudson River to run through.

124
00:06:48,001 --> 00:06:50,980
It's the only place where a major river flows from west

125
00:06:50,981 --> 00:06:53,780
of the Appalachians to the Atlantic.

126
00:06:53,781 --> 00:06:58,080
Nowhere between Birmingham, Alabama on the south

127
00:06:58,081 --> 00:07:00,480
and the St. Lawrence River on the north

128
00:07:00,481 --> 00:07:04,980
Is there a passage through this eastern mountain chain

129
00:07:04,981 --> 00:07:06,600
other than through New York?

130
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:11,140
Nature preordained this path across New York State

131
00:07:11,141 --> 00:07:15,020
back 200 million years ago when the Appalachian Mountains formed.

132
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:18,180
But the Hudson River flowing through this passage

133
00:07:18,181 --> 00:07:19,240
runs north-south.

134
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:23,100
What the nation needed was a connection directly west,

135
00:07:23,720 --> 00:07:25,380
utilizing the Mohawk River Valley,

136
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:28,140
which ran from Albany to central New York State.

137
00:07:28,141 --> 00:07:32,140
The best way to exploit this ancient river route was with a

138
00:07:32,220 --> 00:07:33,040
canal.

139
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:38,960
Water was and still is the cheapest form of

140
00:07:39,020 --> 00:07:41,120
transportation, most economical.

141
00:07:41,180 --> 00:07:44,620
When you look at per ton mile, it takes a lot less energy,

142
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:46,220
and your costs are a lot less.

143
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:47,360
It's a lot smoother.

144
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:51,000
There are no potholes in water like there are in even our

145
00:07:51,060 --> 00:07:53,840
today paved roads, much less the old dirt roads.

146
00:07:55,840 --> 00:07:58,140
In the early 19th century,

147
00:07:58,141 --> 00:08:01,140
engineers considered canals the logical choice when faced with

148
00:08:01,141 --> 00:08:03,140
a daunting transportation challenge.

149
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:09,140
They'd been part of humanity's technological legacy since 4000

150
00:08:09,141 --> 00:08:14,140
BC, when the Egyptian king, Menes, built a canal to unite

151
00:08:14,141 --> 00:08:16,140
Upper and Lower Egypt.

152
00:08:16,141 --> 00:08:19,140
It may have been in canals very much like these that ancient

153
00:08:19,141 --> 00:08:23,140
Egyptians floated blocks of limestone to build the pyramids.

154
00:08:23,141 --> 00:08:29,640
ancient China also forged an enduring legacy of canal

155
00:08:29,641 --> 00:08:30,980
building.

156
00:08:30,981 --> 00:08:35,460
Workers constructed its Grand Canal 1,400 years ago, linking

157
00:08:35,461 --> 00:08:39,500
Beijing, the emperor's capital in the north, with the ocean

158
00:08:39,501 --> 00:08:43,600
going trade of the China coast south of Shanghai.

159
00:08:43,601 --> 00:08:46,700
Commercial traffic moves through it to this day.

160
00:08:49,340 --> 00:08:53,140
By the late 18th century, canals abounded in Europe,

161
00:08:53,141 --> 00:08:55,640
proving their worth as indispensable components of

162
00:08:55,641 --> 00:09:01,140
transportation and commerce. This solid track record wasn't

163
00:09:01,141 --> 00:09:06,180
lost on civic leaders in the fledgling United States.

164
00:09:06,181 --> 00:09:09,600
In 1800, New York City was just another port on the eastern

165
00:09:09,601 --> 00:09:13,100
seaboard. It began to see a canal as the

166
00:09:13,101 --> 00:09:17,100
key to its future success. Well, New York, at the time

167
00:09:17,101 --> 00:09:19,600
that the canal opened, was doing pretty well.

168
00:09:19,601 --> 00:09:22,140
It was growing. The population was growing.

169
00:09:22,141 --> 00:09:26,080
It's the tonnage going in and out of the harbor was increasing.

170
00:09:26,180 --> 00:09:28,040
The value of the goods were increasing.

171
00:09:28,140 --> 00:09:31,180
The only trouble is that Philadelphia was still larger,

172
00:09:31,280 --> 00:09:33,880
and New York was in very big competition with Philadelphia.

173
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:39,200
In 1808, the New York state government

174
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:42,100
began to seriously study the possibility of a canal

175
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:44,660
that would link the Hudson River ports of Manhattan

176
00:09:44,760 --> 00:09:47,100
to the Great Lakes.

177
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:49,940
The following year, a delegation from New York

178
00:09:49,941 --> 00:09:53,280
traveled to Washington, D.C. to request federal funding

179
00:09:53,281 --> 00:09:55,180
for a canal to the west.

180
00:09:55,181 --> 00:09:58,180
They projected a budget of $5 million,

181
00:09:58,181 --> 00:10:00,280
but critics claimed a canal would cost several times

182
00:10:00,281 --> 00:10:01,980
that amount.

183
00:10:01,981 --> 00:10:04,680
So there were many naysayers for a lot of different reasons.

184
00:10:04,681 --> 00:10:10,400
One was an honest-to-goodness feeling and perception

185
00:10:10,401 --> 00:10:12,400
that it would bankrupt the nation,

186
00:10:12,401 --> 00:10:16,400
because we were, at that time, essentially a third world nation.

187
00:10:16,401 --> 00:10:18,140
We went to the federal government,

188
00:10:18,141 --> 00:10:21,740
and Thomas Jefferson said, my God, fellows, come back in

189
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:25,180
about 100 years and maybe we'll talk about it.

190
00:10:25,280 --> 00:10:29,580
In 1810, almost in defiance of Jefferson's flat refusal,

191
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:32,680
New York's leaders launched a feasibility study for a western

192
00:10:32,780 --> 00:10:34,380
waterway.

193
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:37,500
They sent a canal committee to western New York State to

194
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:39,800
examine the possible routes.

195
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:43,100
One of the committee members was a savvy politician named

196
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:45,900
DeWitt Clinton.

197
00:10:45,901 --> 00:10:50,500
In 1817, Clinton won the governorship of New York.

198
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:53,800
He championed the proposed canal, which was projected to cost

199
00:10:53,900 --> 00:10:56,540
more than twice as much as it cost the state to fight the

200
00:10:56,640 --> 00:10:58,040
War of 1812.

201
00:11:00,240 --> 00:11:02,880
Budget-conscious state representatives from Manhattan

202
00:11:02,980 --> 00:11:04,660
adamantly opposed him.

203
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:08,060
They derided his grandiose vision of a canal, dubbing it

204
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:09,160
Clinton's Ditch.

205
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:15,060
In 1817, the canal bill went before the New York legislature.

206
00:11:15,061 --> 00:11:18,260
legislature. By a very narrow margin, the

207
00:11:18,261 --> 00:11:22,560
bill passed. Clinton knew the political

208
00:11:22,561 --> 00:11:25,720
obstacles he had overcome didn't compare to the physical ones that lay

209
00:11:25,721 --> 00:11:30,000
ahead. The Mohawk River Valley through

210
00:11:30,001 --> 00:11:34,420
which the canal would pass was a gauntlet of rugged mountains, icy

211
00:11:34,421 --> 00:11:39,560
rivers, and desolate swamps. The severe topography was the

212
00:11:39,561 --> 00:11:44,420
result of the same natural forces that had carved the gorge.

213
00:11:44,421 --> 00:11:47,720
In a very real way, the Erie Canal

214
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:52,620
began in western New York 12,000 years ago,

215
00:11:52,720 --> 00:11:54,160
geologically speaking.

216
00:11:54,260 --> 00:11:56,660
And at one point, the Great Lakes

217
00:11:56,760 --> 00:12:00,800
flowed almost due east to the Hudson River down what

218
00:12:00,900 --> 00:12:02,480
is now the Mohawk River.

219
00:12:02,580 --> 00:12:06,040
And it was those escaping waters from the Great Lakes

220
00:12:06,140 --> 00:12:11,340
that actually cut the Mohawk River gorge.

221
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:14,400
For the canal's designers, the greatest topic of

222
00:12:14,420 --> 00:12:17,760
created by these ancient waters was a pronounced variation

223
00:12:17,761 --> 00:12:21,460
in elevation between Lake Erie and the Hudson River.

224
00:12:23,700 --> 00:12:28,040
The difference in elevation is 565 feet,

225
00:12:28,041 --> 00:12:32,080
and boats can't sail uphill.

226
00:12:32,081 --> 00:12:35,300
Nowhere was the shift in elevation more extreme than at

227
00:12:35,301 --> 00:12:40,520
the 60-foot high solid limestone Niagara escarpment, which

228
00:12:40,521 --> 00:12:45,520
which creates Niagara Falls, 25 miles to the northwest.

229
00:12:45,521 --> 00:12:48,520
Skeptics claimed constructing a canal over such rugged terrain

230
00:12:48,521 --> 00:12:53,160
would be impossible, but an army of engineers and laborers

231
00:12:53,161 --> 00:12:55,160
was determined to prove them wrong.

232
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:05,520
The total cost to construct the Erie Canal was $7 million,

233
00:13:05,521 --> 00:13:09,180
equivalent to about $3 billion today.

234
00:13:09,181 --> 00:13:12,080
The Erie Canal will return on Modern Marvels.

235
00:13:17,080 --> 00:13:20,420
We now return to the Erie Canal on Modern Marvels.

236
00:13:26,140 --> 00:13:30,340
In the mid-19th century, this site in downtown Buffalo

237
00:13:30,341 --> 00:13:32,240
was filled with water.

238
00:13:32,241 --> 00:13:35,640
It was a man-made harbor called a commercial slip.

239
00:13:35,641 --> 00:13:38,380
The slip was situated at the crucial site where there was

240
00:13:38,381 --> 00:13:41,320
where the western terminus of the Erie Canal met the Great

241
00:13:41,321 --> 00:13:42,380
Lakes.

242
00:13:42,381 --> 00:13:45,320
Here, canal boats that were too small to navigate the lakes

243
00:13:45,321 --> 00:13:48,320
crossed paths with lake vessels that were too large

244
00:13:48,321 --> 00:13:50,820
for the canal.

245
00:13:50,821 --> 00:13:55,140
The commercial slip was the primary connection between

246
00:13:55,141 --> 00:13:58,040
the Erie Canal and the Great Lakes trade.

247
00:13:58,041 --> 00:14:01,840
This was a point where cargoes had to be transferred.

248
00:14:01,841 --> 00:14:05,480
The cargo had to be moved from one type of vessel to another,

249
00:14:05,481 --> 00:14:11,620
And that was a shipment between lakes, schooners, and the canal

250
00:14:11,720 --> 00:14:13,720
boats, the smaller canal boats.

251
00:14:13,820 --> 00:14:16,120
Preservationists are beginning to restore the slip

252
00:14:16,220 --> 00:14:20,120
to its Erie Canal heyday by removing the dirt that's

253
00:14:20,220 --> 00:14:23,140
accumulated here in recent decades.

254
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:25,240
Once the debris is cleared, city officials will fill

255
00:14:25,340 --> 00:14:28,540
the slip with water, enabling boats to dock in downtown

256
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:30,640
Buffalo.

257
00:14:30,740 --> 00:14:34,140
Now, this was a real bustling commercial center.

258
00:14:34,141 --> 00:14:37,640
We're trying to recreate that site, the flavor, the character,

259
00:14:37,740 --> 00:14:40,840
the ambiance of that 19th century site as a way

260
00:14:40,940 --> 00:14:43,780
to encourage tourism here in Buffalo,

261
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:46,660
and really help people learn and understand the role of the Erie

262
00:14:46,760 --> 00:14:49,620
Canal in the history of this country.

263
00:14:49,720 --> 00:14:52,120
At the site, archaeologist Frank Schapatti

264
00:14:52,220 --> 00:14:55,060
has unearthed artifacts that reveal how the canal pioneered

265
00:14:55,160 --> 00:14:57,760
the way west.

266
00:14:57,860 --> 00:14:59,360
Frank Schapatti, Archeologist, Archeologist

267
00:14:59,361 --> 00:15:03,200
that we find probably in the thousands.

268
00:15:03,300 --> 00:15:06,860
There are oyster shells from the East Coast

269
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:12,500
that were shipped in barrels up the canal.

270
00:15:12,600 --> 00:15:15,900
And it excited a lot of the Easterners who had moved here

271
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,080
or were passing through here because they could finally

272
00:15:18,180 --> 00:15:21,680
get fresh oysters.

273
00:15:21,780 --> 00:15:23,420
Beyond fresh Atlantic oysters,

274
00:15:23,520 --> 00:15:25,280
just about every commodity imaginable

275
00:15:25,380 --> 00:15:27,220
once passed through this commercial nucleus.

276
00:15:29,360 --> 00:15:34,700
The Erie Canal made it all possible.

277
00:15:34,701 --> 00:15:37,100
Two centuries ago, the prodigious challenge of designing

278
00:15:37,101 --> 00:15:40,660
a waterway through New York's rugged wilderness fell upon three

279
00:15:40,661 --> 00:15:45,120
determined men. Surveyor James Geddes laid out

280
00:15:45,121 --> 00:15:47,660
the canal's route during a scouting expedition through the

281
00:15:47,661 --> 00:15:51,660
Mohawk River Valley. Geddes was later joined by

282
00:15:51,661 --> 00:15:55,660
geologist Amos Eaton, who later founded Rensselaer Polytechnical

283
00:15:55,661 --> 00:15:59,200
Polytechnical Institute, now one of America's premier

284
00:15:59,201 --> 00:16:02,200
engineering schools.

285
00:16:02,201 --> 00:16:05,800
And heading the entire project was surveyor Benjamin Wright,

286
00:16:05,801 --> 00:16:10,600
hailed by many as the father of American civil engineering.

287
00:16:10,601 --> 00:16:13,320
None of these men were professionally trained

288
00:16:13,321 --> 00:16:17,020
engineers, and they came to that position either as land

289
00:16:17,021 --> 00:16:20,860
surveyors, oftentimes they were trained as mathematicians,

290
00:16:20,861 --> 00:16:24,060
oftentimes they were trained as scientists, but none of them

291
00:16:24,061 --> 00:16:25,300
trained as engineers.

292
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:28,060
And in fact, Benjamin Wright had no college education

293
00:16:28,160 --> 00:16:29,000
whatsoever.

294
00:16:29,100 --> 00:16:31,000
But for that matter, neither did the other two men.

295
00:16:31,100 --> 00:16:33,800
There was a little formal engineering knowledge

296
00:16:33,900 --> 00:16:36,800
in the sense of formal education.

297
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:39,800
But there was a lot of hands-on, practical men,

298
00:16:39,900 --> 00:16:43,040
well-schooled and surveying.

299
00:16:43,140 --> 00:16:44,180
They read widely.

300
00:16:44,280 --> 00:16:46,980
They knew all about pre-existing canals,

301
00:16:47,080 --> 00:16:50,860
especially in England and in France.

302
00:16:50,960 --> 00:16:52,560
The canal's pragmatic designers

303
00:16:52,561 --> 00:16:56,160
construction on the least challenging terrain.

304
00:16:56,260 --> 00:16:58,700
Rather than starting at the rocky and difficult terminals

305
00:16:58,800 --> 00:17:02,040
of Buffalo or Albany, they chose to begin at the center

306
00:17:02,140 --> 00:17:06,300
of the proposed route, on a wide, flat plain in the village

307
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:07,900
of Rome.

308
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:14,420
Town officials broke ground on the 4th of July, 1817.

309
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:17,360
The canal would be little more than a basic dirt ditch,

310
00:17:17,460 --> 00:17:21,520
and its width of only 40 feet and depth of just 4 feet may

311
00:17:21,521 --> 00:17:26,520
made it slightly smaller than its European counterparts.

312
00:17:26,521 --> 00:17:29,520
Builders had determined that four feet of water would supply just

313
00:17:29,521 --> 00:17:35,520
enough buoyancy for a fully loaded canal boat.

314
00:17:35,521 --> 00:17:39,520
And canal commissioners didn't want to pay for anything deeper.

315
00:17:39,521 --> 00:17:42,520
The Canal Commission contracted out the labor to farmers who

316
00:17:42,521 --> 00:17:47,520
lived along the route proposed by the canal's designers.

317
00:17:47,521 --> 00:17:50,520
Typically, most of the diggers, the general maintenance people

318
00:17:50,521 --> 00:17:52,520
maintenance people were unskilled laborers.

319
00:17:52,521 --> 00:17:58,520
The early diggers on the canal were frankly a pretty rough bunch of people.

320
00:17:58,521 --> 00:18:00,520
They were tough.

321
00:18:00,521 --> 00:18:03,520
They were there to make a living.

322
00:18:03,521 --> 00:18:05,520
It was a well-paying job actually.

323
00:18:05,521 --> 00:18:10,520
But a lot of fights, a lot of labor fights, that type of a thing.

324
00:18:10,521 --> 00:18:13,520
These men were people who were used to hard work

325
00:18:13,521 --> 00:18:16,520
and working all day long in all types of weather.

326
00:18:16,521 --> 00:18:18,520
And that's what they faced while they dug the canal.

327
00:18:18,521 --> 00:18:21,920
The canal was dug usually from spring to fall,

328
00:18:22,020 --> 00:18:24,920
and they worked 12 to 16-hour days

329
00:18:25,020 --> 00:18:28,760
clearing the land so they could build the ditch.

330
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:30,760
Essentially, it was from time to time,

331
00:18:30,860 --> 00:18:36,300
2,000, 3,000, perhaps 4,000 men working away at from 50 to 75

332
00:18:36,400 --> 00:18:41,940
cents a day to get this ditch built 363 miles

333
00:18:42,040 --> 00:18:45,080
across the state of New York.

334
00:18:45,180 --> 00:18:47,040
The canal's laborers relied mostly

335
00:18:47,041 --> 00:18:49,440
and shovels and strong backs.

336
00:18:49,540 --> 00:18:51,640
But they also developed innovative new tools,

337
00:18:51,740 --> 00:18:54,980
like the tree stump puller.

338
00:18:55,080 --> 00:18:59,520
The mobile device consisted of two wheels 16 feet in diameter

339
00:18:59,620 --> 00:19:01,480
set on a long axle.

340
00:19:01,580 --> 00:19:03,020
Mounted in the middle of the axle

341
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:05,100
was a slightly smaller spool.

342
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:07,100
A rope was wound around the spool

343
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:09,700
and harnessed to a team of horses.

344
00:19:09,800 --> 00:19:14,100
A chain wound around the axle was secured to a tree stump.

345
00:19:14,101 --> 00:19:18,100
After workers braced the outside wheels, the horses pulled the

346
00:19:18,101 --> 00:19:21,600
rope, which turned the axle, and wound in the chain.

347
00:19:21,601 --> 00:19:26,340
This provided the leverage necessary to extract the stump.

348
00:19:26,341 --> 00:19:29,340
Extracting a stump with a pick and shovel might take a lone

349
00:19:29,341 --> 00:19:33,340
farmer an entire day or more.

350
00:19:33,341 --> 00:19:36,340
Using a tree stump remover, a crew of seven men and four

351
00:19:36,341 --> 00:19:40,760
horses could pull out 40 stumps a day.

352
00:19:40,761 --> 00:19:44,560
Such ingenuity and sheer brawn resulted in a productive first

353
00:19:44,561 --> 00:19:47,960
season of construction. But when the ground froze in

354
00:19:47,961 --> 00:19:52,000
November of 1817, crews had to cease work for the winter.

355
00:19:52,001 --> 00:19:58,320
They had completed 15 miles of Erie Canal, 348 miles, and the

356
00:19:58,321 --> 00:20:03,320
route's most treacherous terrain still remained.

357
00:20:03,321 --> 00:20:07,320
Erie Canal laborers were paid 80 cents a day, nearly double

358
00:20:07,321 --> 00:20:09,820
the wage paid to other unskilled laborers in America

359
00:20:09,821 --> 00:20:13,160
America at that time. The Erie Canal will return

360
00:20:13,161 --> 00:20:22,160
on Modern Marvels. We now return to the Erie Canal

361
00:20:22,161 --> 00:20:31,580
on Modern Marvels. These engineering students

362
00:20:31,581 --> 00:20:35,080
from Union College in Schenectady, New York, are treading

363
00:20:35,081 --> 00:20:38,620
in the footsteps of history. They're restoring a section of

364
00:20:38,621 --> 00:20:41,660
section of the Erie Canal, using only the kinds of tools

365
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:43,860
available to the men who built it.

366
00:20:43,960 --> 00:20:46,360
What better way to learn about the enormity of the challenge

367
00:20:46,460 --> 00:20:49,660
faced by the original workforce?

368
00:20:49,760 --> 00:20:51,260
We could come in here with the backhoats

369
00:20:51,360 --> 00:20:53,460
and scramble things around fairly quickly.

370
00:20:53,560 --> 00:20:55,380
But we want to teach the students what it was like to

371
00:20:55,480 --> 00:20:57,680
build this before you had the major backhoats.

372
00:20:57,780 --> 00:21:00,380
We've got students in their mechanics classes that are

373
00:21:00,480 --> 00:21:02,480
working with pulleys and mechanical advantage and stuff

374
00:21:02,580 --> 00:21:03,680
like that.

375
00:21:03,780 --> 00:21:05,980
And engineering hasn't changed a whole lot.

376
00:21:06,080 --> 00:21:08,620
The laws of physics hasn't changed.

377
00:21:08,621 --> 00:21:11,860
Perhaps no more fitting classroom than this exists

378
00:21:11,960 --> 00:21:15,420
for budding engineers, because the Erie Canal has been called

379
00:21:15,520 --> 00:21:17,960
America's first school of engineering.

380
00:21:20,660 --> 00:21:22,860
The first two seasons of canal construction

381
00:21:22,960 --> 00:21:24,860
had been productive.

382
00:21:24,960 --> 00:21:28,180
In 1817 and 1818, the canal's builders

383
00:21:28,280 --> 00:21:32,280
had dug close to 95 miles of the Erie Canal.

384
00:21:32,380 --> 00:21:35,280
But that early progress had been over the flattest and most

385
00:21:35,380 --> 00:21:38,620
easily excavated terrain of the route.

386
00:21:38,621 --> 00:21:40,920
In 1819, builders would have to cope

387
00:21:41,020 --> 00:21:43,920
with more extreme topography, beginning with New York's

388
00:21:44,020 --> 00:21:46,760
great Montezuma swamp.

389
00:21:46,860 --> 00:21:49,260
In March, when the ground began to thaw,

390
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:53,860
workers began digging through the marshy land west of Syracuse.

391
00:21:53,960 --> 00:21:56,280
Men worked waist deep in the swamp,

392
00:21:56,380 --> 00:21:59,280
flinging out shovelfuls of mud from holes that immediately

393
00:21:59,380 --> 00:22:01,380
filled with mud again.

394
00:22:01,480 --> 00:22:04,680
By July, mosquitoes descended on the workers

395
00:22:04,780 --> 00:22:08,620
and became a bigger problem than the mud.

396
00:22:08,621 --> 00:22:11,420
Malaria, carried by the mosquitoes,

397
00:22:11,421 --> 00:22:14,020
spread rapidly through the workforce.

398
00:22:14,021 --> 00:22:18,660
An estimated 1,000 laborers fell seriously ill one season,

399
00:22:18,661 --> 00:22:20,860
and scores of the afflicted are said to have died.

400
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:26,980
The salvation of the workforce was the early onset of winter

401
00:22:26,981 --> 00:22:28,680
in 1819.

402
00:22:28,681 --> 00:22:31,820
The cold killed the mosquitoes and froze the swamp,

403
00:22:31,821 --> 00:22:34,880
enabling the workers to dig through the firmer terrain

404
00:22:34,881 --> 00:22:37,180
and shore up the canal banks with wood pilings.

405
00:22:38,620 --> 00:22:40,660
Other obstacles the laborers faced

406
00:22:40,760 --> 00:22:43,100
were dozens of streams and rivers,

407
00:22:43,200 --> 00:22:45,120
which crisscrossed the canal route

408
00:22:45,220 --> 00:22:47,600
and had to be either incorporated into the canal

409
00:22:47,700 --> 00:22:49,200
or traversed.

410
00:22:49,300 --> 00:22:50,700
To cross the rivers, the workers

411
00:22:50,800 --> 00:22:54,440
used a tried and true system perfected by the ancient Romans,

412
00:22:54,540 --> 00:22:56,340
the aqueduct.

413
00:22:56,440 --> 00:23:00,480
The purpose of an aqueduct was to take your boat from one side

414
00:23:00,580 --> 00:23:03,380
of a creek or river to the other side.

415
00:23:03,480 --> 00:23:05,740
It did not lift you or drop you down.

416
00:23:05,741 --> 00:23:10,380
The method of holding the water was done by a floor made of

417
00:23:10,480 --> 00:23:14,340
timbers 18 inches thick, and then there were two walls

418
00:23:14,440 --> 00:23:16,280
providing a box.

419
00:23:16,380 --> 00:23:18,480
I like to think of it as a rain gutter.

420
00:23:18,580 --> 00:23:22,260
And all it did was just take you from one side of the creek

421
00:23:22,360 --> 00:23:24,120
to the other side.

422
00:23:24,220 --> 00:23:26,060
The longest of the Erie Canal aqueducts

423
00:23:26,160 --> 00:23:30,740
featured 26 50-foot high arches that spanned more than 1,100

424
00:23:30,840 --> 00:23:33,500
feet of river.

425
00:23:33,501 --> 00:23:36,140
Year after year, mile after mile,

426
00:23:36,200 --> 00:23:38,600
the workers made steady progress,

427
00:23:38,680 --> 00:23:40,640
halting during each winter's freeze,

428
00:23:40,700 --> 00:23:43,240
then resuming at the spring thaw.

429
00:23:43,300 --> 00:23:47,880
They saved the most difficult obstacle for last,

430
00:23:47,940 --> 00:23:51,820
a ridge of solid limestone at the town of Lockport.

431
00:23:51,880 --> 00:23:55,860
Its geological name was the Niagara Escarpment.

432
00:23:55,920 --> 00:23:59,620
One of the challenges that the engineers had

433
00:23:59,621 --> 00:24:03,620
engineers had was getting over the Niagara escarpment.

434
00:24:03,621 --> 00:24:07,460
It held Lake Erie from entering New York State.

435
00:24:07,461 --> 00:24:09,860
And to the north, you'll find Niagara Falls,

436
00:24:09,861 --> 00:24:11,360
which is similar.

437
00:24:11,361 --> 00:24:15,360
There's a 30-foot thick layer of limestone and flint.

438
00:24:15,361 --> 00:24:19,280
The escarpment itself is 60 feet high.

439
00:24:19,281 --> 00:24:21,380
To traverse the Niagara escarpment,

440
00:24:21,381 --> 00:24:24,180
the canal's designers had to devise a way to get water to

441
00:24:24,181 --> 00:24:28,180
flow uphill for 60 feet.

442
00:24:28,181 --> 00:24:29,720
The solution to this daunting problem

443
00:24:29,721 --> 00:24:33,280
had been engineered six centuries earlier by the Dutch.

444
00:24:33,281 --> 00:24:37,020
It was known simply as the lock.

445
00:24:37,021 --> 00:24:40,520
A lock is a device that lifts or lowers boats from one water

446
00:24:40,521 --> 00:24:42,620
level to another.

447
00:24:42,621 --> 00:24:45,440
After a boat heading uphill enters the lock,

448
00:24:45,441 --> 00:24:48,840
the gates are closed, and water flows in,

449
00:24:48,841 --> 00:24:52,180
raising the water line and the vessel.

450
00:24:52,181 --> 00:24:54,840
When the water level reaches that of the elevated segment

451
00:24:54,841 --> 00:24:58,740
The gates are opened, and the boat proceeds.

452
00:24:58,840 --> 00:25:01,280
To move downhill, water is released from the lock,

453
00:25:01,380 --> 00:25:04,780
lowering the waterline and the craft.

454
00:25:04,880 --> 00:25:07,780
Transporting boats up the 60-foot-high Niagara

455
00:25:07,880 --> 00:25:12,700
escarpment required five separate locks in a series.

456
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:16,020
Each lock would raise a boat 12 feet to the next lock,

457
00:25:16,120 --> 00:25:20,040
and finally to the top of the ridge.

458
00:25:20,140 --> 00:25:23,560
But maneuvering one boat upward down all five locks

459
00:25:23,561 --> 00:25:27,300
took more than 40 minutes, so the designers devised history's

460
00:25:27,301 --> 00:25:30,600
first set of side-by-side, or twinned locks,

461
00:25:30,601 --> 00:25:33,800
to accommodate two-way traffic.

462
00:25:33,801 --> 00:25:35,800
There were actually 10 locks.

463
00:25:35,801 --> 00:25:37,800
There was a northern tier and a southern tier,

464
00:25:37,801 --> 00:25:41,820
so they could maintain traffic in each direction at the same

465
00:25:41,821 --> 00:25:44,120
time, up and down.

466
00:25:44,121 --> 00:25:47,020
The water flow at Lockport was controlled by valves

467
00:25:47,021 --> 00:25:50,360
within the wooden gates that separated each lock.

468
00:25:50,361 --> 00:25:52,860
The lock master would go to the top of the gate,

469
00:25:52,861 --> 00:25:56,700
of the gate, which originally was 17 feet, six inches high.

470
00:25:56,701 --> 00:26:01,500
He would use a throw rod, 90 to 180 degrees,

471
00:26:01,501 --> 00:26:03,700
cracking the valves themselves.

472
00:26:03,701 --> 00:26:06,100
When he opened them up in about this position,

473
00:26:06,101 --> 00:26:10,080
water then could flow from the high side into the low side.

474
00:26:10,081 --> 00:26:12,140
When the water level was equalized,

475
00:26:12,141 --> 00:26:15,380
then the lock master, by means of a balance beam,

476
00:26:15,381 --> 00:26:19,360
could actually swing the gates up against the wall on one side

477
00:26:19,361 --> 00:26:22,860
and swing the gates up against the wall on the opposite side.

478
00:26:22,861 --> 00:26:26,460
the boat would then enter or leave the lock itself.

479
00:26:26,560 --> 00:26:29,400
Later in the 20th century, engineers modernized

480
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:33,300
the locks, reducing their number from five to two.

481
00:26:33,400 --> 00:26:37,100
The locks were also equipped with motorized gates.

482
00:26:37,200 --> 00:26:39,480
So these locks, we're going to go through our five stories

483
00:26:39,580 --> 00:26:42,480
high that weigh 50 tons apiece, and they're operated

484
00:26:42,580 --> 00:26:44,540
by a seven horsepower motor.

485
00:26:44,640 --> 00:26:46,180
We're going to go up 25 feet.

486
00:26:46,280 --> 00:26:48,080
There'll be 3 and 1 half million gallons of water

487
00:26:48,180 --> 00:26:49,680
flowing here, all by gravity.

488
00:26:49,780 --> 00:26:51,320
When we first start up in the locks,

489
00:26:51,321 --> 00:26:53,720
They can't leave the water in here too fast,

490
00:26:53,820 --> 00:26:56,900
because it boils up, and it gets too rough for the boats.

491
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,820
So once we reach the halfway point,

492
00:26:59,920 --> 00:27:02,160
then the operator can open up the valves a little bit more,

493
00:27:02,260 --> 00:27:04,260
and the water starts to flow in here a little bit faster.

494
00:27:08,340 --> 00:27:10,480
After workers finished building the locks,

495
00:27:10,580 --> 00:27:13,180
scaling the Niagara Escarpment, they

496
00:27:13,280 --> 00:27:15,080
faced yet another obstacle.

497
00:27:15,180 --> 00:27:17,480
They had to blast a three-mile corridor

498
00:27:17,580 --> 00:27:19,620
through the solid limestone wall that

499
00:27:19,621 --> 00:27:21,520
that blocked the path to Buffalo.

500
00:27:21,620 --> 00:27:24,660
This is a miniature version of what would have been used

501
00:27:24,760 --> 00:27:29,300
by the workmen to drill the blast holes for the lockport

502
00:27:29,400 --> 00:27:30,100
cut.

503
00:27:30,200 --> 00:27:36,040
They would have used a specially tempered steel rod

504
00:27:36,140 --> 00:27:39,440
held by one man, while the other man would hit the top

505
00:27:39,540 --> 00:27:41,740
of the rod with a sledgehammer.

506
00:27:41,840 --> 00:27:48,620
Essentially, strike, turn, strike, turn.

507
00:27:48,621 --> 00:27:50,980
These holes would be filled with black gunpowder

508
00:27:51,080 --> 00:27:53,020
and stuffed with a fuse.

509
00:27:53,120 --> 00:27:55,420
They actually would hire a kid to do it

510
00:27:55,520 --> 00:27:57,020
and have the kid light it because it was felt

511
00:27:57,120 --> 00:27:58,460
that kids had really good reflexes

512
00:27:58,560 --> 00:28:01,660
and they could get out of there faster.

513
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:04,060
Once the explosion shattered the rock,

514
00:28:04,160 --> 00:28:06,140
workers smashed it with sledgehammers

515
00:28:06,240 --> 00:28:10,280
and cleared out the debris with shovels and wheelbarrows.

516
00:28:10,380 --> 00:28:13,240
This laborious system was eventually replaced

517
00:28:13,340 --> 00:28:17,180
by the clever creation of an inventor named Orange Dibble.

518
00:28:17,181 --> 00:28:20,520
He built a derrick that could be powered by just one horse,

519
00:28:20,620 --> 00:28:25,360
but was strong enough to lift one ton of stone in a single load.

520
00:28:25,460 --> 00:28:27,820
These derricks were placed every 70 feet

521
00:28:27,920 --> 00:28:30,600
along the west bank of the Lockport Cut.

522
00:28:30,700 --> 00:28:33,800
And so at the bottom of the cut, the men would blast

523
00:28:33,900 --> 00:28:36,400
and put debris in these buckets.

524
00:28:36,500 --> 00:28:38,500
And then the buckets would be lifted and dropped

525
00:28:38,600 --> 00:28:41,500
on the side of the canal.

526
00:28:41,600 --> 00:28:43,200
Dibble's innovation accelerated

527
00:28:43,300 --> 00:28:45,200
the work on the escarpment.

528
00:28:45,201 --> 00:28:48,260
But it still took laborers two years to carve the three-mile

529
00:28:48,261 --> 00:28:49,940
trench to Buffalo.

530
00:28:49,941 --> 00:28:55,140
They finished in the autumn of 1825.

531
00:28:55,141 --> 00:28:58,620
And it was, in fact, that last bit of work in the Lockport

532
00:28:58,621 --> 00:29:01,520
Cut that delayed the opening of the canal.

533
00:29:01,521 --> 00:29:04,820
And it was that last piece of work, when finished,

534
00:29:04,821 --> 00:29:09,320
that signaled the opening of the entire Erie Canal from Buffalo

535
00:29:09,321 --> 00:29:11,260
to New York City.

536
00:29:11,261 --> 00:29:13,100
After eight years of construction,

537
00:29:13,101 --> 00:29:15,100
the Erie Canal was complete.

538
00:29:15,200 --> 00:29:18,400
The final price tag was $7 million.

539
00:29:18,500 --> 00:29:22,740
At a ceremony in Buffalo on October 26, 1825,

540
00:29:22,840 --> 00:29:26,440
Governor DeWitt Clinton boarded a boat named the Seneca Chief

541
00:29:26,540 --> 00:29:29,220
and began a triumphant journey down the entire length

542
00:29:29,320 --> 00:29:31,820
of the Erie Canal.

543
00:29:31,920 --> 00:29:34,120
Towns along the route celebrated his passage

544
00:29:34,220 --> 00:29:37,300
with parades and festivals.

545
00:29:37,400 --> 00:29:42,560
After nine days and 363 miles, Clinton's ceremonial regatta

546
00:29:42,561 --> 00:29:46,560
Hudson River. When his flotilla reached the New

547
00:29:46,561 --> 00:29:49,800
York City harbor, the governor poured a barrel of water from

548
00:29:49,801 --> 00:29:53,500
Lake Erie into the Atlantic Ocean in a ceremony called the

549
00:29:53,501 --> 00:29:57,200
Wedding of the Waters. The Erie Canal was officially

550
00:29:57,201 --> 00:30:00,120
open. Now the real work of America's new

551
00:30:00,121 --> 00:30:06,020
superhighway could begin. Word of the Erie Canal's grand

552
00:30:06,021 --> 00:30:08,960
opening was relayed from Buffalo to Manhattan by a

553
00:30:08,961 --> 00:30:11,900
by a succession of firing cannons.

554
00:30:11,901 --> 00:30:14,460
The cannons, each placed within earshot of the next

555
00:30:14,461 --> 00:30:16,500
along the canal and the Hudson River,

556
00:30:16,501 --> 00:30:20,200
delivered the message in less than an hour and a half.

557
00:30:20,201 --> 00:30:23,200
The Erie Canal will return on Modern Marvels.

558
00:30:29,120 --> 00:30:32,120
We now return to the Erie Canal on Modern Marvels.

559
00:30:38,960 --> 00:30:42,460
It's hard to imagine, but these seemingly desolate spots that

560
00:30:42,461 --> 00:30:46,740
dot upstate New York were once buzzing with Erie Canal boat

561
00:30:46,741 --> 00:30:47,460
traffic.

562
00:30:47,461 --> 00:30:50,280
If you could visualize that this would be filled with water

563
00:30:50,281 --> 00:30:53,440
four feet deep, and the water would come up to about a foot

564
00:30:53,441 --> 00:30:57,420
below the level of the towpath itself, you'd have boats cruising

565
00:30:57,421 --> 00:31:02,820
both east and west at this point, a lot of activity.

566
00:31:02,821 --> 00:31:04,580
It's only fitting that the New York State

567
00:31:04,581 --> 00:31:08,380
Thruway parallels the canal's original footprint.

568
00:31:08,381 --> 00:31:09,940
In many respects, the Erie Canal

569
00:31:09,941 --> 00:31:13,580
was America's first highway, complete with gnarled traffic

570
00:31:13,581 --> 00:31:15,620
and rest stops.

571
00:31:15,621 --> 00:31:18,480
What happened was, as the boats came up to these locks,

572
00:31:18,481 --> 00:31:20,280
it took time to go through.

573
00:31:20,281 --> 00:31:24,200
The little towns blossomed around the lock systems

574
00:31:24,201 --> 00:31:25,940
because of the stalled traffic.

575
00:31:25,941 --> 00:31:28,820
At every lock, there's going to be a general store.

576
00:31:28,821 --> 00:31:30,820
There's going to be a tavern.

577
00:31:30,821 --> 00:31:34,020
There's going to be a butcher shop.

578
00:31:34,021 --> 00:31:35,580
Like any well-traveled thoroughfare,

579
00:31:35,581 --> 00:31:38,580
had its share of fender benders.

580
00:31:38,581 --> 00:31:41,280
There were always accidents, bumping locked gates,

581
00:31:41,281 --> 00:31:44,180
bumping locked doors, hitting the stonework,

582
00:31:44,181 --> 00:31:46,080
hitting each other.

583
00:31:46,081 --> 00:31:49,220
In 1826, the first full year of the Erie Canal's

584
00:31:49,221 --> 00:31:53,240
operation, the concept of traffic was a little different

585
00:31:53,241 --> 00:31:55,240
than it is today.

586
00:31:55,241 --> 00:31:58,240
At any given time, about 160 boats were traveling

587
00:31:58,241 --> 00:32:00,740
on the canal.

588
00:32:00,741 --> 00:32:03,080
Modest as that figure sounds today,

589
00:32:03,081 --> 00:32:05,380
It was proof then that the canal was a triumph

590
00:32:05,480 --> 00:32:06,880
for the young nation.

591
00:32:06,980 --> 00:32:09,180
With the opening of the Erie Canal,

592
00:32:09,280 --> 00:32:12,220
here was something that not only New Yorkers who had paid

593
00:32:12,320 --> 00:32:15,620
for the canal could be proud of, but the whole nation

594
00:32:15,720 --> 00:32:18,320
was proud of this canal.

595
00:32:18,420 --> 00:32:21,260
When the canal first opened, people from all over the world,

596
00:32:21,360 --> 00:32:23,140
but particularly from the eastern United States,

597
00:32:23,240 --> 00:32:24,540
came up to see it.

598
00:32:24,640 --> 00:32:28,180
I mean, it was touted as being this amazing achievement.

599
00:32:28,280 --> 00:32:30,940
There were technological feats that people thought

600
00:32:30,941 --> 00:32:32,980
would be impossible to accomplish.

601
00:32:33,080 --> 00:32:36,380
And so people went out to see it.

602
00:32:36,480 --> 00:32:39,420
Spectators crowding the banks enjoyed a close-up glimpse

603
00:32:39,520 --> 00:32:42,420
of the flat-bottomed boats moving methodically east and

604
00:32:42,520 --> 00:32:44,420
west.

605
00:32:44,520 --> 00:32:48,620
Based upon European models, the boats were usually 14 feet

606
00:32:48,720 --> 00:32:51,200
wide and 78 feet long.

607
00:32:51,300 --> 00:32:56,240
Each could carry 140,000 pounds of freight, most commonly wheat,

608
00:32:56,340 --> 00:33:00,140
lumber, and raw materials from the west, and furniture

609
00:33:00,141 --> 00:33:03,180
and furniture, tools, clothes, and manufactured goods

610
00:33:03,181 --> 00:33:06,680
from the east.

611
00:33:06,681 --> 00:33:08,380
If this was 1850, this boat would

612
00:33:08,381 --> 00:33:11,480
be filled in the middle section with cargo, either boxed up

613
00:33:11,481 --> 00:33:13,880
or in barrels or in sacks.

614
00:33:13,881 --> 00:33:15,780
So this section would be filled from the floor

615
00:33:15,781 --> 00:33:18,960
to the ceiling with a lot of cargo.

616
00:33:18,961 --> 00:33:21,400
Tolls, based on the weight of the cargo,

617
00:33:21,401 --> 00:33:26,260
were calculated by waymasters in so-called waylock buildings.

618
00:33:26,261 --> 00:33:28,460
The system is essentially actually

619
00:33:28,461 --> 00:33:33,460
like an off-ramp on the thruway, on the highway system,

620
00:33:34,160 --> 00:33:35,360
where the trucks would come off.

621
00:33:35,361 --> 00:33:38,080
They come off the canal into the chamber.

622
00:33:38,081 --> 00:33:40,040
The gates are shut behind them.

623
00:33:40,041 --> 00:33:42,960
The water is drained out of the chamber.

624
00:33:42,961 --> 00:33:44,240
Once the water's out of the chamber,

625
00:33:44,241 --> 00:33:47,060
the boat's resting on a cradle in the bottom,

626
00:33:47,061 --> 00:33:50,100
and the boat's weight is pushing that cradle down.

627
00:33:50,101 --> 00:33:53,220
The weight of the boat is then recorded on a scale,

628
00:33:53,221 --> 00:33:55,780
so the waymaster is able to then record

629
00:33:55,781 --> 00:33:57,180
the loaded weight of the boat.

630
00:33:57,181 --> 00:34:00,580
They determine their toll and off the boat goes.

631
00:34:00,581 --> 00:34:05,380
The average tariff was about a half cent per ton per mile.

632
00:34:05,381 --> 00:34:08,120
With his boat fully loaded, a merchant paid a toll of around

633
00:34:08,121 --> 00:34:12,280
$165 for the entire trip.

634
00:34:12,281 --> 00:34:16,580
The Erie Canal became a cash cow for the state of New York.

635
00:34:16,581 --> 00:34:19,280
It was such a success, it paid for all of its construction,

636
00:34:19,281 --> 00:34:22,080
all of the operation, all of the maintenance,

637
00:34:22,081 --> 00:34:24,380
and showed a profit so large in 10 years

638
00:34:24,381 --> 00:34:27,440
that it offset the state budget by two-thirds.

639
00:34:27,441 --> 00:34:29,320
A sizable portion of the canal's revenue

640
00:34:29,321 --> 00:34:31,940
came from passengers paying by the mile

641
00:34:31,941 --> 00:34:35,120
aboard so-called packet boats.

642
00:34:35,121 --> 00:34:38,120
The spirit of their journey was captured in a memorable song.

643
00:34:38,121 --> 00:34:41,720
15 miles on the Erie Canal, she's

644
00:34:41,721 --> 00:34:44,120
a good old worker and a good old worker.

645
00:34:44,121 --> 00:34:46,440
Many enjoyed the journey sitting on the boat's wide

646
00:34:46,441 --> 00:34:49,440
roof and watching the scenery roll by.

647
00:34:49,441 --> 00:34:52,100
But they had to stay alert, because as a cost saving

648
00:34:52,101 --> 00:34:55,700
the bridges over the Erie Canal were built just four feet high.

649
00:34:55,800 --> 00:34:58,700
Boat pilots routinely warned passengers with a cry,

650
00:34:58,800 --> 00:35:02,040
low bridge, everybody down, to remind passengers

651
00:35:02,140 --> 00:35:03,740
to duck as they passed beneath them.

652
00:35:03,840 --> 00:35:07,740
Low bridge, everybody down.

653
00:35:07,840 --> 00:35:10,760
Low bridge, cause we're coming to a town

654
00:35:10,860 --> 00:35:12,360
and you always know you're now.

655
00:35:12,460 --> 00:35:15,460
Many avoided this drama by relaxing inside the boat's

656
00:35:15,560 --> 00:35:16,460
central cabin.

657
00:35:16,560 --> 00:35:18,360
I'm the Erie Canal.

658
00:35:18,460 --> 00:35:20,260
When night fell, passengers converted

659
00:35:20,261 --> 00:35:22,560
converted the boat's interior to sleeping rooms

660
00:35:22,660 --> 00:35:25,460
with pull-down berths.

661
00:35:25,560 --> 00:35:29,500
The sexes were segregated when it came time to sleep.

662
00:35:29,600 --> 00:35:32,400
Men and boys would be on one side of the curtain,

663
00:35:32,500 --> 00:35:34,700
and women would be on the other side.

664
00:35:34,800 --> 00:35:38,020
So almost every canal boat would have a curtain like this,

665
00:35:38,120 --> 00:35:42,620
which would keep things decent during the nighttime.

666
00:35:42,720 --> 00:35:45,020
Canal passengers valued only one thing

667
00:35:45,120 --> 00:35:48,420
more than comfortable lodging.

668
00:35:48,421 --> 00:35:54,960
I got a mule and her name is South, 15 miles on the Erie Canal.

669
00:35:55,560 --> 00:35:58,740
The tireless mule team propelling them toward their destination.

670
00:35:59,760 --> 00:36:01,940
15 miles on the Erie Canal.

671
00:36:01,941 --> 00:36:10,480
The thing that made the canal so successful was the fact that two animals this size could carry loads as great as 75 ton on a canal boat,

672
00:36:10,860 --> 00:36:13,280
as opposed to just a few ton on a wagon.

673
00:36:13,281 --> 00:36:17,080
The mule's abiding companions were the young boys leading

674
00:36:17,180 --> 00:36:20,080
them mile after mile along the towpath.

675
00:36:20,180 --> 00:36:22,020
The boys who worked along the canal

676
00:36:22,120 --> 00:36:25,320
were often sheltered in barns alongside their horses,

677
00:36:25,420 --> 00:36:27,720
often with just four or five hours of sleep a night.

678
00:36:27,820 --> 00:36:30,100
And then they would pull a boat, and then they would stop and

679
00:36:30,200 --> 00:36:33,200
maybe have a few more hours of sleep.

680
00:36:33,300 --> 00:36:36,640
Some 30,000 other workers, including boatmen,

681
00:36:36,740 --> 00:36:39,040
lock tenders, toll takers, and repairmen,

682
00:36:39,140 --> 00:36:43,280
helped establish the Erie Canal as a booming commercial route.

683
00:36:43,281 --> 00:36:46,780
transformed America. Towns along the route, such as

684
00:36:46,781 --> 00:36:50,820
Albany, Rochester, and Syracuse, saw their populations explode

685
00:36:50,821 --> 00:36:54,820
almost overnight. They were just basically very

686
00:36:54,821 --> 00:36:57,720
few inhabitants until the canal came through.

687
00:36:57,721 --> 00:37:01,440
It brought people, brought... They had to stay in places.

688
00:37:01,441 --> 00:37:05,840
They had to buy items. And it was quite an economic

689
00:37:05,841 --> 00:37:10,440
boom, tremendous boom. The Erie Canal transformed

690
00:37:10,441 --> 00:37:12,940
transformed not only Western New York State,

691
00:37:12,941 --> 00:37:15,580
but also New York City.

692
00:37:15,581 --> 00:37:17,880
The canal's steady shipments of flour and grain

693
00:37:17,881 --> 00:37:20,960
made New York the premier port of the United States

694
00:37:20,961 --> 00:37:24,620
and its capital of commerce.

695
00:37:24,621 --> 00:37:26,580
Despite the canal's success, there

696
00:37:26,581 --> 00:37:29,160
were those who opposed it.

697
00:37:29,161 --> 00:37:31,960
They rebelled against the way it transformed the economy

698
00:37:31,961 --> 00:37:35,040
and accelerated the pace of life.

699
00:37:35,041 --> 00:37:37,940
Some people just didn't like the whole market revolution

700
00:37:37,941 --> 00:37:39,500
that was happening at the time.

701
00:37:39,501 --> 00:37:40,880
They were suspicious of it.

702
00:37:40,881 --> 00:37:44,120
They thought that it was leading to class divisions in society.

703
00:37:44,121 --> 00:37:48,060
They thought that it was making people too materialistic.

704
00:37:48,061 --> 00:37:51,440
No matter which side of the issue Americans stood on,

705
00:37:51,441 --> 00:37:54,940
nothing could stop the radical march of progress.

706
00:37:54,941 --> 00:37:58,740
The changes wrought by the canal would be so great that the canal

707
00:37:58,741 --> 00:38:01,860
itself would have to be remade if it were to survive.

708
00:38:06,300 --> 00:38:09,260
To lessen water turbulence that could erode the Erie Canal's

709
00:38:09,261 --> 00:38:14,060
earthen walls, officials set the canal's first legal speed limit

710
00:38:14,061 --> 00:38:18,140
at 4 miles per hour. The Erie Canal will return

711
00:38:18,141 --> 00:38:19,500
on Modern Marvels.

712
00:38:24,780 --> 00:38:27,740
We now return to the Erie Canal on Modern Marvels.

713
00:38:34,300 --> 00:38:37,980
The Day-Peckinpah Barge has been a fixture on the Erie Canal

714
00:38:37,981 --> 00:38:39,540
since 1921.

715
00:38:39,640 --> 00:38:42,620
Today, it's passing through the canal's modernized locks,

716
00:38:42,720 --> 00:38:45,120
just as it has thousands of times in the past.

717
00:38:47,420 --> 00:38:48,820
But this trip is special.

718
00:38:48,920 --> 00:38:50,520
The ship is headed to Albany, where

719
00:38:50,620 --> 00:38:53,120
it will undergo a major transformation from cargo

720
00:38:53,220 --> 00:38:55,840
carrier to historical landmark.

721
00:38:55,940 --> 00:39:01,480
It'll be restored, and it'll be a floating museum classroom.

722
00:39:01,580 --> 00:39:03,580
And we hope to then be able to take it

723
00:39:03,680 --> 00:39:07,980
on the waters of New York State and use it as a public goodwill

724
00:39:07,981 --> 00:39:14,220
tour, educational tour, and to bring people back to the canal system again.

725
00:39:14,221 --> 00:39:20,320
Preservationists felt compelled to save the Day Peckinpah from the scrap heap because

726
00:39:20,321 --> 00:39:23,546
it's the oldest surviving Erie Canal cargo ship.

727
00:39:23,547 --> 00:39:26,100
The Day Peckinpah would never have fit

728
00:39:26,101 --> 00:39:27,355
in Clinton's ditch.

729
00:39:27,356 --> 00:39:31,980
The supersized canal it now traverses was created because the original

730
00:39:31,981 --> 00:39:34,820
was unable to keep pace with a modernizing America.

731
00:39:37,360 --> 00:39:41,660
By 1836, the Erie Canal was hailed as an unqualified success

732
00:39:41,661 --> 00:39:44,960
for New York and for America.

733
00:39:44,961 --> 00:39:48,220
But success brought its own problems.

734
00:39:48,221 --> 00:39:50,960
It didn't take very long into the history of the canal

735
00:39:50,961 --> 00:39:53,960
for people to start complaining about how they had to wait

736
00:39:53,961 --> 00:39:56,800
when a lock was taking too long, when a boat turned over.

737
00:39:56,801 --> 00:39:59,740
The canal, by being so successful, in fact,

738
00:39:59,741 --> 00:40:03,440
in fact, raised the expectations so high that people were soon

739
00:40:03,441 --> 00:40:05,620
quite disappointed with it.

740
00:40:05,621 --> 00:40:08,580
Beginning in 1836, New York officials

741
00:40:08,581 --> 00:40:11,680
launched an improvement project, enlarging the canal

742
00:40:11,681 --> 00:40:15,420
to accommodate bigger boats in greater numbers.

743
00:40:15,421 --> 00:40:17,500
It went from 40 feet wide to 70 feet wide,

744
00:40:17,501 --> 00:40:20,960
from 4 feet deep to 7 feet deep, took out a whole bunch of loops

745
00:40:20,961 --> 00:40:25,760
of Clinton's ditch, made the canal 15 miles, 13 miles shorter.

746
00:40:25,761 --> 00:40:28,800
The tonnage of each boat went from 70 to 240.

747
00:40:28,801 --> 00:40:33,280
That lowered the cost of transportation even more.

748
00:40:33,281 --> 00:40:36,980
By 1862, the enlargement project was complete.

749
00:40:36,981 --> 00:40:38,640
But the canal's increased capacity

750
00:40:38,641 --> 00:40:41,080
served only to attract more traffic,

751
00:40:41,081 --> 00:40:43,240
and congestion continued.

752
00:40:43,241 --> 00:40:46,520
In 1895, officials began yet another improvement

753
00:40:46,521 --> 00:40:49,260
in widening project.

754
00:40:49,261 --> 00:40:52,460
But it was underfunded and poorly planned.

755
00:40:52,461 --> 00:40:57,260
Laborers halted their work when it was only 40% complete.

756
00:40:57,261 --> 00:40:58,820
All the while, the beleaguered canal

757
00:40:58,821 --> 00:41:03,460
was being challenged by a formidable rival, the railroad.

758
00:41:03,461 --> 00:41:05,800
A train could carry more freight and passengers

759
00:41:05,801 --> 00:41:10,480
faster and over longer distances than any canal.

760
00:41:10,481 --> 00:41:13,080
By the 1890s, many declared that the Erie Canal

761
00:41:13,081 --> 00:41:14,440
had outlived its usefulness.

762
00:41:17,180 --> 00:41:21,820
New York's energetic governor, Theodore Roosevelt, disagreed.

763
00:41:21,821 --> 00:41:25,820
In 1899, two years before he would ascend to the presidency,

764
00:41:25,821 --> 00:41:28,620
he took bold action to ensure the Erie Canal's survival.

765
00:41:31,660 --> 00:41:35,960
Roosevelt appointed a commission to study the state's waterways.

766
00:41:35,961 --> 00:41:40,460
This resulted in a $101 million bond measure passed by New York

767
00:41:40,461 --> 00:41:42,920
state voters in 1903.

768
00:41:42,921 --> 00:41:45,880
It called for the construction of an even larger canal,

769
00:41:45,881 --> 00:41:49,980
tailored for 20th century industry.

770
00:41:49,981 --> 00:41:54,220
The so-called barge canal was a far cry from Clinton's ditch.

771
00:41:54,221 --> 00:41:59,860
It was 125 feet wide, 12 feet deep, and lined with concrete.

772
00:41:59,861 --> 00:42:03,220
Vessels on the canal could carry up to 1,600 tons,

773
00:42:03,221 --> 00:42:06,040
and were powered by motors rather than mules.

774
00:42:06,041 --> 00:42:08,540
And engineers installed huge new locks

775
00:42:08,541 --> 00:42:11,580
with electric-powered doors.

776
00:42:11,581 --> 00:42:13,840
But progress had its price.

777
00:42:13,841 --> 00:42:16,700
The barge canal basically erased the engineering marvel

778
00:42:16,701 --> 00:42:18,620
that preceded it.

779
00:42:18,621 --> 00:42:21,620
The portions of the Erie that weren't incorporated

780
00:42:21,621 --> 00:42:24,420
into the barge canal system were abandoned by the state.

781
00:42:24,421 --> 00:42:27,460
In some areas, they remained abandoned up to today.

782
00:42:27,461 --> 00:42:29,660
And then in some sections, the canal

783
00:42:29,661 --> 00:42:32,000
has been incorporated into the barge canal system.

784
00:42:32,001 --> 00:42:35,900
They literally superimposed the barge canal on top of it.

785
00:42:35,901 --> 00:42:38,740
Over the next half century, the modernized canal

786
00:42:38,741 --> 00:42:42,340
became the prime freight route for the state of New York.

787
00:42:42,341 --> 00:42:46,020
But by the 1950s, the combined might of the railroads,

788
00:42:46,021 --> 00:42:49,340
highways, and larger seaways rendered the canal

789
00:42:49,341 --> 00:42:52,480
commercially unviable.

790
00:42:52,481 --> 00:42:57,280
Today, however, the Erie Canal is experiencing a rebirth.

791
00:42:57,281 --> 00:42:59,980
Cities along the route have refurbished sections of the old

792
00:42:59,981 --> 00:43:04,780
waterway to recreate the bygone spirit of its heyday.

793
00:43:04,781 --> 00:43:08,300
Replicas of old canal boats take sightseers on tours from Albany

794
00:43:08,301 --> 00:43:11,000
to Buffalo.

795
00:43:11,001 --> 00:43:14,000
The canal is also enjoying its new image as a recreational

796
00:43:14,001 --> 00:43:15,900
attraction.

797
00:43:15,901 --> 00:43:18,800
The New York State Canal Corporation has refashioned

798
00:43:18,801 --> 00:43:22,140
the old mule towpath for bikers and runners

799
00:43:22,240 --> 00:43:25,900
and opened the canal to water sports.

800
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:27,140
We're seeing more kayaks.

801
00:43:27,240 --> 00:43:28,440
We're seeing more canoes.

802
00:43:28,540 --> 00:43:31,140
Who would have ever thought canoes and kayakers,

803
00:43:31,240 --> 00:43:32,540
you know, going through logs?

804
00:43:32,640 --> 00:43:34,940
The future of the canal is bright, I think,

805
00:43:35,040 --> 00:43:39,660
because this waterway can be used in so many different ways.

806
00:43:39,760 --> 00:43:42,300
It's ironic that now this canal that we celebrate,

807
00:43:42,400 --> 00:43:45,360
because it seems like it represents a slower pace of life,

808
00:43:45,361 --> 00:43:49,060
It seems as though it was a time when life was a lot less

809
00:43:49,160 --> 00:43:49,960
complicated.

810
00:43:50,060 --> 00:43:52,460
In fact, at the time, it was seen as being something that

811
00:43:52,560 --> 00:43:54,160
was complicating people's lives.

812
00:43:54,260 --> 00:43:56,000
And that was a sign of progress and in no way

813
00:43:56,100 --> 00:43:58,300
a sign of the past.

814
00:43:58,400 --> 00:43:59,600
Well, I think that the Erie Canal,

815
00:43:59,700 --> 00:44:01,740
very much like any technological advances

816
00:44:01,840 --> 00:44:04,420
that we see today, did have the effect of raising

817
00:44:04,520 --> 00:44:05,820
people's expectations.

818
00:44:05,920 --> 00:44:08,480
And so I think we can see that with anything

819
00:44:08,580 --> 00:44:09,920
in our own lifetimes.

820
00:44:10,020 --> 00:44:12,160
It was well with plane transportation

821
00:44:12,260 --> 00:44:14,660
or most recently with the internet, for example.

822
00:44:14,661 --> 00:44:18,060
The Erie Canal, created in the formative years

823
00:44:18,160 --> 00:44:21,960
of the Republic, endures as a symbol of American ingenuity

824
00:44:22,060 --> 00:44:25,400
and the pursuit of progress.

825
00:44:25,500 --> 00:44:28,600
It remains a treasured institution

826
00:44:28,700 --> 00:44:32,020
that conjures rich images of the nation's past.

827
00:44:32,120 --> 00:44:34,620
For those who ply its waters today,

828
00:44:34,720 --> 00:44:38,120
the refrain of the classic ballad may still ring true.

829
00:44:38,220 --> 00:44:41,520
Coming to the town and you'll always know your neighbor.

830
00:44:41,620 --> 00:44:43,460
You'll always know your pal.

831
00:44:43,461 --> 00:44:48,460
If you ever navigated on the Erie Canal.

